\section{Method}\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Preliminaries}\label{sec:background}

\paragraph{Goal Accessibility Ratio.}
Standard metrics such as perplexity and attention entropy capture generation quality but not \emph{what} the model attends to. In multi-turn dialogue, a model may generate fluent text while progressively ignoring task specifications in the system prompt. We need a metric that directly measures whether goal-relevant context receives attention. We denote the task specification tokens in the system prompt as \emph{goal tokens} $\mathcal{G}$. In the standard transformer attention mechanism \citep{vaswani2017attention}, \citet{dongre2026attention} defined the Goal Accessibility Ratio (GAR) for a model with $L$ layers and $H$ heads at turn $\tau$ as
\begin{equation}\label{eq:gar}
  \text{GAR}(\tau) = \frac{1}{L \cdot H \cdot |\mathcal{R}_\tau|} \sum_{\ell=1}^{L} \sum_{h=1}^{H} \sum_{i \in \mathcal{R}_\tau} \sum_{j \in \mathcal{G}} A_{i,j}^{(\ell,h)},
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{R}_\tau$ is the set of response tokens at turn $\tau$ and $A_{i,j}^{(\ell,h)}$ is the attention weight from token $i$ to token $j$ at layer $\ell$, head $h$.

\paragraph{Two GAR variants.}
\textbf{GAR-Dongre} is the original definition (Eq.~\ref{eq:gar}), averaging over all response tokens. However, averaging dilutes the signal with intermediate positions that may legitimately attend to local context rather than the system prompt. The last token of each assistant turn is where the model aggregates information for the next continuation---attention at this position most directly reflects the model's awareness of task specifications. \textbf{GAR-ours} restricts the measurement accordingly:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:gar-ours}
  \text{GAR\text{-}ours}(\tau) = \frac{1}{L \cdot H} \sum_{\ell=1}^{L} \sum_{h=1}^{H} \sum_{j \in \mathcal{G}} A_{t_\tau,\, j}^{(\ell,h)},
\end{equation}
where $t_\tau$ is the last token index at turn $\tau$. Attention at this position reflects the information drawn upon for continuation; we use GAR-ours as the optimization target for $\mathcal{L}_\text{GAR}$ (\S\ref{sec:loss}).

\subsection{ThreadGate Architecture}

Different transformer layers serve different roles: early layers capture local syntax while later layers integrate long-range context \citep{gromov2024unreasonable}. A global intervention would uniformly scale all layers, but goal attention decay is layer-dependent: some layers contribute more to cross-turn information routing than others. ThreadGate therefore adds a \emph{per-layer} gating mechanism to the residual stream, allowing the model to learn which layers to modulate. At each layer $l$, the residual update is scaled by a scalar gate:
\begin{equation}
  x_{l+1} = x_l + g_l \cdot f_l(x_l),
\end{equation}
where $f_l$ denotes the sublayer computation (self-attention or feed-forward network, FFN). From the perspective that residual networks function as ensembles of shallow paths \citep{veit2016residual}, per-layer gating selectively modulates individual paths. This resembles ReZero \citep{bachlechner2021rezero}, which initializes a single trainable scalar at zero to stabilize deep network training. ThreadGate differs in three ways: (i)~gates are \emph{content-conditioned}, a function of the hidden state rather than a fixed scalar; (ii)~gates are \emph{turn-conditioned}, incorporating conversation-position features; (iii)~gates are initialized near identity ($\sigma(1.5) \approx 0.82$) rather than zero, preserving the pretrained model's computation.

\paragraph{Gate network.} A fixed scalar per layer (as in ReZero) cannot adapt to varying inputs; the gate must be \emph{content-conditioned} so that different conversations or turns can receive different modulation. Adding \emph{turn conditioning} allows the gate to distinguish early turns (where GAR is naturally high) from late turns (where intervention is most needed). Each gate $g_l$ is thus produced by a two-layer MLP that takes both content and turn features as input:
\begin{align}\label{eq:gate}
  g_l = \sigma\bigl(W_2\,\text{ReLU}(&W_1\,[\text{proj}(x_l);\;\text{emb}(t_d, t_i)] \notag\\
  &+ b_1) + b_2\bigr),
\end{align}
where $\text{proj}(\cdot)$ reduces the hidden dimension to 16, and $\text{emb}(t_d, t_i)$ provides a 16-dimensional turn embedding. The 32-dimensional input passes through a 64-unit hidden layer to a scalar gate in $(0, 1)$ via sigmoid. For Qwen-2.5-7B ($L\!=\!28$), this adds ${\sim}$57K parameters ($<$0.001\% of 7B).

\paragraph{Turn embedding.} The embedding $\text{emb}(t_d, t_i)$ encodes turn distance $t_d$ (turns since system prompt) and turn index $t_i$ via learned lookup tables, allowing gates to distinguish conversation positions. The \textbf{Uniform Gate} ablation replaces $\text{emb}(\cdot)$ with a learnable constant, removing turn awareness.

\paragraph{Initialization.} Gate biases are set to 1.5 (${\sim}$82\% pass-through), contrasting ReZero's zero initialization that would suppress pretrained capabilities. Gate parameters receive a $10\times$ learning rate multiplier.

\subsection{Training Objective}\label{sec:loss}

Next-token prediction alone provides no gradient signal for attention allocation; a model can achieve low perplexity while distributing attention arbitrarily. The auxiliary GAR loss provides explicit pressure to maintain goal-token attention, while a diversity regularizer prevents gate collapse. The training objective combines three terms:
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal{L} = \mathcal{L}_\text{NTP} + \lambda_\text{GAR}\,\mathcal{L}_\text{GAR} + \lambda_\text{div}\,\mathcal{L}_\text{div}.
\end{equation}
$\mathcal{L}_\text{NTP}$ is the standard next-token prediction loss. We use mean squared error (MSE) rather than cross-entropy for $\mathcal{L}_\text{GAR}$ because GAR is a continuous ratio, not a probability, and MSE provides \emph{bidirectional} pressure, penalizing both under-attention (GAR\,$<$\,$\tau$) and over-attention (GAR\,$>$\,$\tau$). This symmetry is critical: a unidirectional maximize objective drives gates toward identity (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-loss}). The GAR loss is:
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal{L}_\text{GAR} = \text{MSE}(\text{GAR\text{-}ours}, \tau),
\end{equation}
where $\tau = 0.5$ targets allocating half of the last token's attention capacity to goal tokens. GAR is measured at the last token of each assistant turn (GAR-ours, \S\ref{sec:background}). Without explicit diversity pressure, all gates converge to the same value (typically near 1.0), eliminating the layer-specific modulation that is the design intent. The gate diversity loss prevents this collapse:
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal{L}_\text{div} = \text{ReLU}(\sigma_\text{min} - \text{std}(\mathbf{g})),
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{g} = [g_1, \ldots, g_L]$ and $\sigma_\text{min}$ is a minimum standard deviation threshold. Both $\lambda_\text{GAR} = \lambda_\text{div} = 1.0$.

\paragraph{Loss design evolution.} The MSE formulation was selected from seven iterations spanning four loss families (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-loss}): cumulative (v1--v2) and maximize (v3--v6) objectives either starve gate gradients at equilibrium or drive gates toward identity. Only MSE provides bidirectional pressure, penalizing both over- and under-attention, preventing identity collapse.

\subsection{Controlled Comparison Design}\label{sec:comparison-design}

We design a five-way comparison where each adjacent pair isolates one factor: \textbf{ThreadGate (TG) vs.\ Uniform Gate (UG)} (turn conditioning), \textbf{TG vs.\ LoRA-only} (gating mechanism), \textbf{ThreadGate vs.\ +PCGrad} (gradient direction conflict via gradient surgery \citep{yu2020pcgrad}), and \textbf{Fine-tuned vs.\ Base} (LoRA adaptation). All fine-tuned variants share training data, LoRA configuration, optimizer, and seed (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-hyper}).

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig5_attention_steering.pdf}
  \caption{Inference-time $\beta$-steering recovers GAR from 0.064 to 0.727, confirming attention misallocation as the proximal cause (9\% PPL cost at $\beta$\,=\,3; phase transition at $\beta \in [2, 3]$).}
  \label{fig:steering-recovery}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig4_attention_heatmap.pdf}
  \caption{Per-turn GAR-ours under AR generation ($N$\,=\,5 conversations, 10 turns). All fine-tuned variants cluster together (77--81\% decay; $\Delta$\,=\,3.3\,pp between ThreadGate and LoRA-only); Base decays 87.5\%. Gating adds zero incremental effect.}
  \label{fig:attention-heatmap}
\end{figure}
